// #189 (PERF-HOF-CARRIER) reproducer, committed per R15.
//
// The `R8 W8 jit-aliased-cow-push` refusal protects against a real SEGFAULT:
// MIR lowering emits `Operand::Move` for a `let` binding, the JIT nulls the
// source slot after the read, and a later `.push()` on that slot dereferences
// NULL. The refusal is UNCHANGED for that shape.
//
// What changed in #189 is the question it asks. It used to scan the WHOLE
// function for any `Move` of the receiver slot, which its own doc-comment
// called out as over-conservative — and that conservatism was not free. It is
// what kept `arr.map(|x| ..)` out of the JIT entirely: the map desugaring
// pushes into a result accumulator and then Moves it out, so a Move always
// exists AFTER every push. It now asks whether a Move can REACH the push
// (CFG reachability, back-edges included), because a Move that cannot reach
// the push cannot null the slot before it.
//
// Measured at 26f10bb8 BEFORE the fix, every group below produced
// `program_fallback: jit-compile-error — R8 W8 jit-aliased-cow-push SURFACE`
// and ran entirely on the interpreter. After: no fallback, identical output.
//
// The true-positive control for the refusal is NOT here, because it cannot be
// written as a Shape program: `let alias = data; data.push(4)` is rejected by
// the move checker ([B0005] "cannot use this value after it was moved") in
// both modes before it reaches the JIT. It is proven directly against
// hand-built MIR in `mir_compiler::v2_array::aliased_cow_push_reachability_tests`
// (`a_move_before_the_push_in_the_same_block_still_refuses` and
// `a_move_after_the_push_in_a_loop_still_refuses`).
//
// This file's job is the VM==JIT anchor for the shapes the narrowing newly
// admits.

// (1) map: push into an accumulator, then move the accumulator out.
let xs = [1.0, 2.0, 3.0]
let doubled = xs.map(|x| x * 2.0)
print(doubled)

// (2) push first, alias afterwards — the Move is strictly after the push and
//     no path returns to it.
var after: Array<int> = [1, 2, 3]
after.push(4)
let alias_after = after
print(alias_after)

// (3) pushes in a loop, alias only once the loop has finished.
var looped: Array<int> = [1, 2, 3]
var i = 0
while i < 3 {
    looped.push(i)
    i = i + 1
}
let alias_looped = looped
print(alias_looped)
